[{:seon.task/name "a-first-fork-waits-for-nothing-it-does-not-use"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-11-08: \"Slow tests should be treated as bugs and fixed\"; every process test pays a root's boot and its first agent. Profiled 2026-11-08 (load 16-27, so inflated; the ratios hold): before the boot fork server serves its first :fork it decodes all of main's indexes (seon.db/preload!, 3.2-4.7 s), builds main's reach graph and reconciler index, compiles main's tests (`seon.program/load-all!`, 2-3 s), builds the wiki's FTS5 text index and warms an agent's first write (`seon.fork-server/warm-start`, 2.2-3.1 s), and freezes the heap (`seon.lib/static!`, 0.4-0.7 s): about 70% of a first agent's wait, and a scripted test agent uses almost none of it. Minimum: a fork costs a fork (~70 ms). What exists now: the boot server loads main's tests and preloads the indexes at its own start (`seon.fork-server/main`), and root's boot asks the server's :preload once its import is written (`seon.fork-server/preload`: tests, graph, index, warm-start, static), so a fork after it costs a fork; but a :fork that comes before the :preload is served waits for all of it inline (`seon.fork-server/server-step`'s :fork arm runs the same pieces first), and :preload is one step, not steps between requests. Plan: the boot server forks agents at once and warms in steps between requests (each a message to itself: graph, index, text index, tests by namespace), and an agent forked before a piece is warm makes that piece itself when it first uses it (`seon.agent/base-graph`, `seon.agent/reconcile-index`, text search, its prove's test load), as derived data regenerable from the db at t (docs/data-modeling.md#derived-data-is-a-cache-never-relied-on); a later fork inherits it warm. Also read the reach graph from the kept program store where bin/test keeps it (`test-runner/reach-graph-of`: a sixth of a build's ticks) rather than building it. Proofs: a cost proof that a root's first agent is ready within a fork's work plus its own start, flat in the program's size; seon.agent-test/an-agent-holds-mains-tests-and-runs-them still green (an agent that runs tests finds them, loading them itself when it came first). Wiki: docs/machine.md#an-agents-first-write. Measured 2026-11-10 (Optimization; notes first-agent-plan-2026-11-10.md): a root's first agent waits 4.5 s (preload! 1.2, test load 1.1, reach graph 0.8, text index 0.7); later forks cost 20-54 ms. Under load the boot server's warm-up runs ~35 s at ~98% CPU, reading nothing meanwhile: that made every stop of a young root take 26-32 s, fixed apart (jolt/faststop: the stop ends the server's group), but a first :fork still waits it out. The design: the boot server queues its warm steps as messages to itself in its own recv! (decode, the tests by namespace, graph, index, static) and serves a frame from root first, a warm step only when none waits; root's requests never wait behind a step longer than one namespace's load. Then keep what is per program by its digest (Point's question): the test load against the binary's warm-tests by each namespace's forms' digest (~1 ms a compare), the reach graph by the program digest beside the store (50-150 ms read), the FTS5 text index as a file by the wiki's content digest (1-5 ms open); preload! is decode work, Deep fixes'. Approved apart (Point, 2026-11-10): warm-start's per-agent part (begin-run-tx speculated, the task's ctx built: 33-41 ms of every attach) moved off the fork path, keeping the shared part once a move of main, ON CONDITION that seon.agent-test/an-agents-start-costs-the-same-however-long-its-root-has-run stays green with its bound unmoved."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fork-server-test/root-s-first-agent-ready-within-fork-s}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-answer-pairs-by-request-and-stop-ends-a-wedged-server"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-25 by the stability sweep (docs/failure-points.md), checked against the code; a lost server's waits are answered already (`seon.fork-server/fork-server-proc`'s :lost answers its :waiters). Both parts read true on 2026-11-06. (1) :stats and :static answers pair with :waiters by FIFO (`peek`). After a promote the standby is sent every unanswered request again, so FIFO holds only while every answer the dead server wrote arrived whole before its EOF; an answer cut by the death pairs the next waiter with the wrong request. The request carries its :rid to the server and the answer echoes it, and the proc answers the waiter by :rid. (2) `seon.fork-server/release!` waits for the boot server's exit with a blocking `c-waitpid` (no WNOHANG), so a wedged server (a deadlocked child, docs/failure-points.md#physics-who-may-run-threads) hangs root's stop; then `.join 5000` on the event reader is a clock deciding (docs/clocks.md#if-nothing-has-changed-we-do-nothing). Stop is a kill of the server's process group (as a root's processes end with it, docs/machine.md#processes) followed by the reap and the reader's EOF, and no timeout. Deletes the join's timeout; replaces FIFO pairing with :rid pairing."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/a-promoted-servers-answers-pair-by-request-id seon.procs-test/stop-ends-a-wedged-fork-server}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-tx-step-that-threw-on-a-map-says-which"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen once 2026-11-05 in the standing store's problems before its erase, not reproduced: the fork server's step threw on :tx, nth not supported on this type: PersistentArrayMap. The likely site, by reading only: `seon.fork-server/server-step`'s :tx arm takes (nth op 1) and (nth op 2) of each of the message's :tx-data (for `seon.doc/carry-index!`), which assumes a datom or a vector; an entry that is a map throws. The writer's notice is typed as `:seon.db/resolved-tx` (a vector of [op e a v] tuples, resources/seon/schemas/seon.core.edn; `seon.writer/stored!`), so a map should be refused where it is made; whether that contract is checked on that send in a running root is not verified. The step's failure answers and the server serves on, so what is lost is that commit's application to this server's main until the next. Correct: find which writer sends a map in :tx-data (or show the contract refuses it), and make the message's shape a contract so the class is refused where it is made. Proof to write: the step given a commit whose tx-data the writer really sends."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fork-server-test/commit-tx-data-holds-map-entry-refused-where}}
{:seon.task/name "an-agent-inherits-the-section-leaves-it-searches"
  :seon.task/plan "Not ready: the fork server's resident-memory cost against bytes saved per agent is unmeasured; Optimization measures and proposes to the owner Sean. Measured 2026-11-08 (an agent's first search, seon.agent-test/an-agents-first-search-reads-the-index-it-inherited): its first grep allocated 8-15 MB more than its second, all cold decoding of the EAVT leaves holding the sections' :seon.doc/text, which every forked agent decodes for itself; the text index itself is inherited current. Candidate: read those leaves in seon.fork-server/warm-start, so each agent inherits them decoded (copy-on-write). Measure first: the fork server's resident memory with and without it (docs/machine.md's budget), against the bytes saved per agent times agents per hour; propose to the owner with the numbers. Not started."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-fork-step-that-throws-answers-every-waiting-ask"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-09 (jolt/cite-ref, a process test): the fork server's step threw on a :fork (```seon.fork-server/server-step```; the cause then was ``seon.ctx/watched`` answering nil for a reverse :by join with no value, fixed on that branch), root recorded :seon.fork-server/step-failed, :seon.agent/failed and :seon.agent/died, and the test's in-agent ask (``seon.agent-test/in-agent``, an attach waiting on the agent the fork owed) was never answered: the shard sat 15+ min at 0% CPU until killed. Reproduction: any :fork step that throws while an ask waits on that agent (make the fork step throw on a test root and attach). Evidence: root's fiber dump (kill -INFO) at ``/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-sean-src-seon-next/e3d9a2c9-b188-4792-866b-0f8b0ffe1d7e/scratchpad/hang-root.log``: root and the fork server parked on alt-handler, no fiber holding the ask's answer. Cause, as far as known: the step's failure is answered to the fork's own requester (a platform problem and the agent's death) but not to the asks that wait on the agent being made. Correct: the throw answers every waiting ask with the error, as `seon.repl/defect-answer` does for the render proc (every wait ends with its value or a typed error: docs/errors.md#nothing-crashes-and-nothing-hangs). Proof, red first: ``seon.fork-server-test/a-fork-step-that-throws-answers-the-waiting-attach``."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fork-server-test/fork-step-throws-answers-attach-waiting-agent-error}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-commit-is-applied-while-the-fork-server-warms"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: Optimization. Seen 2026-11-11 on the standing system's page after batch 83: \"fork lag 23952 ms\" on an idle system. Measured: the lag (seon.fork-server's :applied, fork_server.clj ~201: now minus the writer's :ns at its send, writer.clj ~77) of the boot's 10 commits was 23,952 to 19,534 ms, falling: they queued behind the fork server's boot work (its preload and warm-up) and were applied in order as it drained; the tile's p50 of a 200-commit window (seon.system ~788, ~1773) then stays frozen at that backlog, since an idle system commits nothing more. Two defects: (1) main's first commits wait ~24 s behind the warm-up, so an agent forked meanwhile may fork from a main the fork server has not yet applied (measure at the next boot: the fork's :t against main's); (2) the stat reads the boot's backlog forever. Correct: the fork server applies main's commits as they come while it warms (the warm-up is not one step that holds its inbox), and the tile reads the lag of the commits since the boot settled (none: none). Proof: at a boot whose warm-up takes N ms, a commit made during it is applied within its own apply time, not N."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fork-server-test/a-commit-made-while-the-fork-server-warms-is-applied-at-once}}
]
